== Data Sources

=== 2020 Decennial Census of Population and Housing

The first source of data for this project comes from the 2020 Decennial Census. Mandated by the Constitution of the United States, the census is carried out every 10 years by the U.S. Census Bureau and "determines the number of people living in the United States" @censusAbout. Though the primary purpose of the census is to distribute seats in the U.S. House of Representatives @censusAbout, it is also the official source of high level demographic and housing data for the entire country. As such, it is used as the source of truth in this project. Every single model will be compared to the values provided by the 2020 Decennial Census to evaluate the quality of predictions. It is important to note that, though treated as the source of truth, that the values provided by the U.S. census are not perfect. An analysis performed by the U.S. Government Accountability Office in 2024 found 14 states exhibiting statistically significant net coverage errors in the 2020 census, though Iowa is absent from this list @censusUncertainty. Accounting for this uncertainty is outside the scope of this project, so all values provided by the census are treated as the true values. 

=== American Community Survey

The second, and primary source of data for this project comes from the American Community Survey (ACS). The ACS is an ongoing survey performed by the U.S. Census Bureau about the population of the United States of America @acsAbout. The Census Bureau is primarily known for the U.S. Decennial Census, but the lesser known ACS serves as a crucial companion piece to it. Instead of every 10 years, the ACS is sent out to a sample of addresses every single month of every single year @acsVsCensus. The ACS in this aspect fills a very important role, as it is able to provide current information between decennial census collections, and is also able to cover a more robust set of topics than the census. The tradeoff is that the ACS comes from a sample of households, so every result is met with a certain degree of uncertainty. However, unlike traditional surveys, individuals selected for the ACS are legally obligated to respond to it @acsFAQ. The consequence of this is that the uncertainty in the ACS results can be treated as a known quantity, which allows for mathematical techniques that are not normally possible with traditional surveys that are optional to respond to. 

The data from the ACS comes in two primary forms, 1-year and 5-year estimates. The 1-year estimates are less precise but more current, encompassing 12 months of collected data but only providing information on areas with a population size of at least $65,000$ @acsYearVersions. The 5-year estimates are based on 60 years of collected data, and so are more precise but less current. The 5-year estimates also provide information on all areas surveyed, so this even includes extremely sparsely populated county or census tract level data @acsYearVersions. This project will be using the 2020 5-year estimates, encompassing survey data from 2016-2020. 

=== NASS Crop Data Layer

The third source of data for this project is known as the Crop Data Layer, or CDL. According to the USGS, the CDL, created by the USDA National Agricultural Statistics Service (NASS), is "an annual raster, geo-referenced, crop-specific land cover data layer produced using satellite imagery and extensive agricultural ground reference data" @cdlUSGS. The USDA website carries direct downloads to every annual data layer going back to 2008. The year used for this analysis is the same as the decennial census, 2020. This dataset contains per-pixel cropland classifications at a 30-meter resolution for the entire country. The classifications were done using a machine learning model trained primarily on the Farm Service Agency (FSA) Common Land Unit (CLU) data, though some states incorporate additional auxiliary data from other agencies and institutions @cdlUSGS. This data within the region of Iowa is used to create the spatial covariate for the Fay-Herriot model. Incorporation of pixel classification uncertainty in the CDL is outside the scope of this project, so all classifications are treated as the true classifications. 